Stored credential connectivity checks: retain hostname identity when pinning
=========================================================================

Resolving and pinning a device hostname protects the outgoing socket from a
second DNS lookup. It must not replace the identity requested by the operator.
Previously the stored-credential HTTP check used the pinned IP for both the
connection and TLS verification, and sent that IP in the HTTP Host header.
This rejected valid hostname certificates and broke virtual-host routing. It
could also accept a certificate valid only for the IP when the operator had
requested a hostname, demonstrating a different certificate identity check.

For a hostname resolved to a different pinned literal, the endpoint now sets
the original hostname and appropriate port in the Host header, and uses HTTPX's
documented sni_hostname extension for HTTPS. HTTPX normalizes the hostname for
these fields. The actual request URL still contains only the validated pinned
private IP. Certificate verification remains enabled by default; private CA
files/directories, explicit TLS opt-out and direct proxy-free routing remain.
SNI is also supplied under explicit TLS opt-out because servers may use it for
virtual-host selection even when the client is not verifying the certificate.

Plaintext fallback retains the original Host header but remains explicitly
opt-in. A completed HTTPS status never triggers fallback; redirects remain
unfollowed. Header-only streaming and the shared elapsed transport deadline
are unchanged. A 2xx response remains a connectivity observation, not proof of
successful authentication. Direct IP-literal request behavior is unchanged.

Qualification uses real PostgreSQL, actual system hostname resolution and owned
HTTP/TLS listeners. Local runners bind an owned hosts file in a private mount
and network namespace; container checks use Docker's hostname mapping. There
is no resolver, HTTPX transport or security-validator replacement in the new
cases. This proves hostname identity and routing, not adversarial DNS rebinding.
The existing security validation and pinned socket URL are retained.

Twenty-eight regression cases reproduce twelve failures with sixteen controls.
They cover trusted hostname certificates, explicit TLS opt-out, IP-literal
controls, rejection of wrong-name and untrusted certificates, rejection of an
IP-only certificate for a hostname request, explicit HTTP fallback, no default
fallback, no fallback after an HTTPS error, redirects, and permission/target
denials. Servers inspect TLS SNI, HTTP Host and synthetic Basic Authentication.
Each API request checks all 51 existing credential rows. Separate incoming TCP
HTTP probes complement the ASGI regression requests and use the same real
outgoing transport. Local and shipping qualification have distinct receipts.

Only the stored-credential test function changes. No frontend, OpenAPI, database
schema, historical rows or existing test assertions are changed. IPv6 literal
formatting/hostname-to-IPv6 behavior, concurrent authority revocation and full
vendor-specific authentication remain separate work. No customer devices are
contacted, and this correction is not enterprise-readiness certification.

HTTPX documents this mechanism at:
https://www.python-httpx.org/advanced/extensions/
